<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rímac River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rímac is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s central coast that flows from the Andes through the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> to the Pacific at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>. Its valley held the Maranga complex of the Lima culture and later shrines of the Ychsma, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded Lima on its south bank in 1535. Its name, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> for 'speaker', is linked to an oracle in the valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rimac-river.jpg" alt="Rímac River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerio de Defensa del Perú · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MINISTRO_DE_DEFENSA_SOBREVOL%C3%93_LIMA_PARA_SUPERVISAR_ACATAMIENTO_DE_AISLAMIENTO_SOCIAL_OBLIGATORIO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
